        "id": 1897307
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-22T14:40:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I haven\u0027t looked at mkfs code, but the Disk layout description says \n\n\"For the special case of block group 0, the first 1024 bytes are unused, to allow for the installation of x86 boot sectors and other oddities. The superblock will start at offset 1024 bytes, whichever block that happens to be (usually 0). However, if for some reason the block size \u003d 1024, then block 0 is marked in use and the superblock goes in block 1. For all other block groups, there is no padding.\"\n\n\nAnd I would 100% like to avoid the possibility of the magic string remaining in the disk because that can lead to data being erased on next VM run. What do you think?\n\nI dont think the writes can be racy because we wait for this function to complete before running mkfs",
      "parentUuid": "27098614_b49c9b42",
      "revId": "029ff79068646723da111dac48de3b6d703403ad",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
